 product of numbers in an iterable, preserving integer type if no floats are present.\"\"\"\n        result = 1\n        has_float = False\n        for v in iterable:\n            if isinstance(v, float):\n                has_float = True\n            result *= v\n        return float(result) if has_float else int(result)\nclass UniqueProductResult:\n    \"\"\"Data transfer object (DTO) to encapsulate the result of the unique product calculation.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, product: Number, unique_count: int, timestamp: datetime.datetime):\n        self.product = product\n        self.unique_count = unique_count\n        self.timestamp = timestamp\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"Converts the UniqueProductResult object into a dictionary for serialization.\"\"\"\n        return {\n            \"product\": self.product,\n            \"unique_count\": self.unique_count,\n            \"timestamp\": self.timestamp.isoformat(),\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, d: dict) -> \"UniqueProductResult\":\n        \"\"\"Creates a UniqueProductResult instance from a dictionary representation.\"\"\"\n        return cls(\n            product=d[\"product\"],\n            unique_count=d[\"unique_count\"],\n            timestamp=datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(d[\"timestamp\"])\n        )\nDEFAULT_CACHE_DIR = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(\"~\"), \".cache\", \"unique_product\")\nDEFAULT_CACHE_FILENAME = \"last_result.json\"\nclass ResultRepository:\n    \"\"\"Manages the optional persistence of the last computed result to a local file.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, cache_path: Optional[Path] = None):\n        \"\"\"Initializes the repository, ensuring the cache directory exists.\"\"\"\n        p = Path(cache_path) if cache_path else Path(DEFAULT_CACHE_DIR) / DEFAULT_CACHE_FILENAME\n        self.cache_path = p\n        self.cache_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    def save(self, result: UniqueProductResult) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Saves the provided UniqueProductResult object to the configured cache file as JSON.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            with open(self.cache_path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                json.dump(result.to_dict(), f)\n        except IOError:\n            # IO errors during saving are caught and suppressed to prevent failure of main logic.\n            pass\n\n    def load(self) -> Optional[UniqueProductResult]:\n        \"\"\"Loads the last UniqueProductResult from the cache file, returning None if not found or corrupted.\"\"\"\n        if not self.cache_path.exists():\n            return None\n        try:\n            with open(self.cache_path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                d = json.load(f)\n            return UniqueProductResult.from_dict(d)\n        except (json.JSONDecodeError, KeyError, ValueError, IOError):\n            # Errors during loading (e.g., file corruption) are caught and suppressed.\n            return None\nclass InputValidator:\n    \"\"\"Validates the input iterable for type, maximum length, and element types.\"\"\"\n    MAX_LEN = 1_000_000\n\n    def validate(self, numbers: Iterable[object]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Performs comprehensive validation on the input iterable of numbers, raising InvalidInputError on failure.\"\"\"\n        if not self._is_iterable(numbers):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be an iterable of numbers.\")\n\n        # For Sized iterables like lists/tuples, check length efficiently.\n        if isinstance(numbers, (list, tuple)):\n            if len(numbers) > self.MAX_LEN:\n                raise InvalidInputError(f\"Input too large (>{self.MAX_LEN} elements).\")\n\n        # Iterate through elements to ensure all are numeric.\n        for idx, item in enumerate(numbers):\n            if not NumberUtils.is_number(item):\n                raise InvalidInputError(f\"Element at index {idx} is not numeric: {item!r}\")\n\n    def _is_iterable(self, value: object) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Internal helper to determine if a value is iterable.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            iter(value)\n            return True\n        except TypeError:\n            return False\nclass UniqueProductCalculator:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the core logic for deduplicating numbers and computing their product.\"\"\"\n    def compute(self, numbers: Iterable[Number]) -> Number:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the product of unique numeric values from the input iterable.\"\"\"\n        uniques = self._unique(numbers)\n        if not uniques:\n            return 1  # The multiplicative identity for an empty set.\n        return NumberUtils.product(uniques)\n\n    def _unique(self, numbers: Iterable[Number]) -> List[Number]:\n        \"\"\"Extracts and returns a list of unique numeric values from the input, preserving order of first appearance.\"\"\"\n        seen = set()\n        uniques = []\n        for n in numbers:\n            # Numeric equality handles comparisons like 1 == 1.0.\n            if n not in seen:\n                seen.add(n)\n                uniques.append(n)\n        return uniques\nclass UniqueProductService:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates the entire process: input validation, unique product calculation, and optional result persistence.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, validator: InputValidator, calculator: UniqueProductCalculator, repository: Optional[ResultRepository] = None):\n        \"\"\"Initializes the service with instances of its dependencies.\"\"\"\n        self.validator = validator\n        self.calculator = calculator\n        self.repository = repository\n\n    def calculate(self, numbers: Iterable[Number]) -> Number:\n        \"\"\"Executes the calculation pipeline: validates input, computes product, and optionally persists the result.\"\"\"\n        # Convert the iterable to a list to allow multiple passes (e.g., by validator and calculator).\n        nums_list = list(numbers)\n\n        self.validator.validate(nums_list)\n        product = self.calculator.compute(nums_list)\n\n        if self.repository:\n            try:\n                # Store the result, including the count of unique numbers.\n                result = UniqueProductResult(product=product, unique_count=len(set(nums_list)), timestamp=datetime.datetime.utcnow())\n                self.repository.save(result)\n            except Exception:\n                # Any exceptions during persistence are caught to ensure the main computation completes successfully.\n                pass\n        return product\n_default_service = UniqueProductService(\n    validator=InputValidator(),\n    calculator=UniqueProductCalculator(),\n    repository=ResultRepository()  # Enables optional result persistence by default.\n)\ndef unique_product(numbers: Iterable[Number]) -> Number:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of unique numbers in a given list.\n\n    This function validates the input, deduplicates numbers by value, computes their product,\n    and optionally saves the result. Invalid inputs raise an InvalidInputError.\n    \"\"\"\n    return _default_service.calculate(numbers)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "Mbpp/580": [{"task_id": "Mbpp/580", "solution": "def extract_even(t: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Recursively removes odd integer elements from a nested tuple, preserving structure.\n    Empty nested tuples formed after filtering are omitted from the result.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_elements = []\n    for item in t:\n        if isinstance(item, int):\n            if item % 2 == 0:\n                result_elements.append(item)\n        elif isinstance(item, tuple):\n            # Recursively process sub-tuple\n            sub_tuple_result = extract_even(item)\n            # Only include non-empty sub-tuples in the result\n            if sub_tuple_result:\n                result_elements.append(sub_tuple_result)\n    return tuple(result_elements)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status"